        {
          "say": "Ten squared is 100, so log base 10 of 100 is 2.",
          "write": "log₁₀ 100 = 2"
        },
        {
          "say": "The base is positive and not 1. And x must be positive.",
          "write": "a > 0, a ≠ 1, x > 0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Evaluating logs",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Log base 2 of 32. Two to what power gives 32? Five.",
          "write": "2⁵ = 32 → log₂ 32 = 5"
        },
        {
          "say": "Log base 10 of 0.01. That's one hundredth, which is ten to the minus 2.",
          "write": "0.01 = 10⁻² → −2"
        },
        {
          "say": "A number less than 1 has a negative log."
        },
        {
          "say": "Log base 8 of 4. Write both as powers of 2.",
          "write": "8 = 2³,  4 = 2²"
        },
        {
          "say": "Two cubed to the y equals two squared. So three y equals 2.",
          "write": "3y = 2 → y = ⅔"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Special values",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Log of 1 is always 0, because a to the 0 is 1.",
          "write": "logₐ 1 = 0"
        },
        {
          "say": "Log of the base itself is 1, because a to the 1 is a.",
          "write": "logₐ a = 1"
        },
        {
          "say": "Log of a to the n is just n.",
          "write": "logₐ (aⁿ) = n"
        },
        {
          "say": "The calculator's log key is base 10 only.",
          "write": "log = log₁₀"
        },
